<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="section-5-3">
  <title>Worksheet 5.3, Diagonalization</title>

  <page>

    <!-- Exercise 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-5-3-ex-1" workspace="2in"><introduction>
      <p> Recall from lecture: a matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if it can be written as <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement><p><m>P</m> is a matrix whose columns are __________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p><m>D</m> is a __________________ matrix.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>The elements on the main diagonal of <m>D</m> are __________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>A diagonal matrix is a matrix that __________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>The geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue is _____________________________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>A matrix can be diagonalized when the geometric multiplicities of all eigenvalues _______________________________________________.</p></statement></task></exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-5-3-ex-2" workspace="2in"><introduction>
      <p> If possible, construct <m>P</m> and <m>D</m> so that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>. Eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are given.</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement>
          <p>
            <m>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
              3 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
              1 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 \\
              1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            \lambda = 2, 2, 5
            </m>
          </p>
        </statement></task><task><statement>
          <p>
            <m>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 &amp; 3 \\
              4 &amp; 2
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            \lambda = -2, 5
            </m>
          </p>
        </statement></task><task><statement>
          <p>
            <m>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
              3 &amp; 2 \\
              0 &amp; 3
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            \lambda = 3, 3
            </m>
          </p>
        </statement></task></exercise>

</page>
<page>

    <!-- Exercise 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-5-3-ex-3" workspace="2in"><introduction>
      <p> If possible, give an example of:</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement><p>a singular <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix in echelon form that can be diagonalized.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>a singular <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix in echelon form that cannot be diagonalized.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>an invertible <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix in echelon form that can be diagonalized.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>an invertible <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix in echelon form that cannot be diagonalized.</p></statement></task></exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 4 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-5-3-ex-4" workspace="1in"><introduction>
      <p> Indicate whether the statements are true or false.</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement><p>If <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, then so is <m>A^2</m>.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If <m>A^2</m> is diagonalizable, then so is <m>A</m>.</p></statement></task></exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 5 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-5-3-ex-5" workspace="2in"><statement>
      <p> Give an example of an upper triangular <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix <m>A</m> such that <m>0</m> is its only eigenvalue and its eigenspace is <m>3</m>-dimensional.
      Explain why the eigenspace has dimension <m>3</m>.</p>
    </statement></exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>